<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bari</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bari is a port city on the Adriatic coast of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. The seat of an Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> from about 847 to 871 and later of the Byzantine catepan of Italy, it fell to the Normans in 1071. The relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a>, brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myra">Myra</a> in 1087, made its basilica a major pilgrimage centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bari.jpg" alt="Bari" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bari_-_Lungomare_Imperatore_Augusto_%26_Mare.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 835 Aghlabid forces campaigned in Calabria and Apulia, taking Amantea, Taranto and <b>Bari</b>, but the destruction of a Muslim fleet off Ostia in 849 halted the advance, and Malta was conquered in 870.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Roger subdued Capua, <b>Bari</b> and Naples, and in 1139 his son captured Innocent II at Galluccio; by the Treaty of Mignano the pope then recognised Roger as king of Sicily, duke of Apulia and prince of Capua.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1087 merchants from <b>Bari</b> carried his relics to their city.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>The Greek citizens of <b>Bari</b> rose against the Lombard rebels led by Melus and delivered the city to Basil Mesardonites, the Byzantine catepan of Italy, who entered it and re-established Byzantine authority.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-11">11 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Led by the local Lombard Melus, an insurrection broke out in <b>Bari</b> against the Catepanate of Italy, the Byzantine authority based there, and quickly spread to other cities.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-9">9 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Byzantines occupied <b>Bari</b> in 876, and it became the capital of the Theme of Longobardia.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-bari">Emirate of Bari</a></li></ul></section>
